          "normalized_title": "Caltech Hosts Workshop on Data Centers and Community Impact for Local Leaders",
          "normalized_summary": "On June 15, Caltech brought together local government officials, researchers, and community stakeholders at the Resnick Sustainability Center for a workshop on the growing impact of AI data centers on California communities. \"Data centers are not your typical one-off economic development project. They are super context dependent, as you'll hear about today. What's a good data center in one area might be a bad data center in another area,\" said Sara Kassir, research policy lead at Caltech's Ronald and Maxine Linde Center for Science, Society, and Policy (LCSSP), in her introductory remarks as facilitator of the data centers and community impact workshop. Organized by the LCSSP, the Resnick Sustainability Institute, and the Caltech Science Exchange, the event was designed to connect decision-makers directly with scientific perspectives. The workshop reflects a broader initiative for Caltech to serve as a resource for local communities and to build relationships and shared understanding that can strengthen local capacity for more coordinated regional responses. Invitees included representatives from nearly two dozen organizations across the San Gabriel Valley and Greater Los Angeles region, including city governments, water districts, public health agencies, and environmental advocates. Six presentations offered perspectives on the national data center debate, California's policy and legal landscape, community health and infrastructure impacts, and public opinion. Presentations were followed by open discussion, giving local officials the opportunity to raise questions and share firsthand experience, ensuring that the research conversation was grounded in the realities that communities are facing. Data centers are projected to account for up to 12 percent of US electricity consumption by 2028. Local governments are often the first entities to see proposals for major projects like these and have very little state and federal guidance on data centers to handle them, share",
